# Environment — ignore all .env files so a future .env.production can't be
# committed by accident. .env.development is already tracked (CORS-only); keep an
# explicit example checked in if one is ever added.
.env*
!.env.development
!.env.example

# Local notes
ANONYMIZE-*.md
CLAUDE.md
REVIEW-REMEDIATION-*.md

# The wasm bridge's build output is COMMITTED, not ignored.
#
# `packages/web/src/lib/wasm/index.ts` imports it statically, so every build
# path needs it: the Dockerfile's frontend stage, `nix build`, CI, and a fresh
# clone. None of those has a Rust toolchain with the wasm32 target and a
# `wasm-bindgen-cli` pinned to the exact version in Cargo.lock, and a mismatched
# CLI emits glue that does not match the module. Ignoring it meant every build
# off a clean checkout failed to resolve the import, which is how it reached
# trunk. `make wasm` regenerates it; generated/SOURCE records what it was built
# from, and a test fails if the two have drifted.

# Generated test fixtures. `cargo test -p tend-wasm` writes these (see
# crates/tend-wasm/tests/fixtures.rs) with a freshly minted age identity each
# run, so they are build output, not source. They were tracked once, which put
# a live AGE-SECRET-KEY in the repository — a throwaway one that guards nothing,
# but a secret key in a repo regardless, and the file churned on every test run.
crates/tend-wasm/tests/fixtures/
